Step‐by‐Step Reasoning

1. Overview of the Mission  
 • The mission is to have one or more robots collect resources from storage and then deliver them to a requester.  
 • The overall mission decomposes into two major phases: Collection and Delivery.  
 • In the Collection phase, the robot (a) computes an optimal “storage order” (based on waiting time plus path length), (b) sends a precise resource request to the storage and waits for retrieval, and (c) if its battery falls below 10% during collection, it must go to recharge and reassign the mission.  
 • In the Delivery phase, after the resource is retrieved the robot must deliver it (possibly in multiple “runs”) to the specified location. However, if its battery drops below 30% during delivery, it must return the resource to a checkpoint and reassign the remaining delivery. In case returning to the checkpoint fails, an alert is triggered and a report is sent to the sector manager.  
 • Moreover, if several resources are requested (from different storages) the design supports multiple robots working in parallel on separate “resource missions” to reduce overall mission time.

2. Hierarchical Decomposition  
 • At the highest level we define an “Achieve” goal (G1) whose target condition is that “all required resources are delivered as specified.”  
 • Under G1, we create one or more resource missions (for example G1.1 for Resource 1, G1.2 for Resource 2, etc.). When multiple items are needed these missions will be executed in parallel (using the “#” symbol).  
 • Each resource mission (G1.1, …) further decomposes into two main phases: Collection (G1.1.1) and Delivery (G1.1.2). Both phases are required (AND relationship) so that a resource mission succeeds only when both phases are completed successfully.  
 • In the Collection Phase (G1.1.1), we distinguish:  
  – G2.1: A Query goal that “computes” the optimal storage order (querying estimated waiting times and path lengths).  
  – G2.2: A Perform goal that “sends a request” and waits for retrieval.  
  – G2.3: A fallback Perform goal that “returns to recharge and reassign” the mission when the battery is low (<10%).  
  The runtime annotation is chosen as “FALLBACK((G2.1; G2.2), G2.3)” because if the battery is not low the robot first computes the order then performs the resource request; if battery is low, it takes the fallback branch.  
 • In the Delivery Phase (G1.1.2), we similarly have:  
  – G3.1: A Perform goal that “delivers” the resource.  
  – G3.2: A fallback Perform goal that is triggered when the battery is low (<30%). This fallback is itself decomposed: first the robot must return the resource to a checkpoint (G3.2.1) and then reassign the remaining delivery (G3.2.2). If even that fails, an alert is triggered and a report is sent (G3.2.3).  
  The runtime annotation is “FALLBACK(G3.1, (G3.2.1; G3.2.2) FALLBACK(G3.2.3))” meaning that normally the delivery is performed, but if battery is low a sequential fallback is executed, with an extra fallback if returning to the checkpoint fails.  

3. Task Decomposition  
 • Each actionable leaf goal is associated with a concrete task that can be directly assigned to a robot.  
 • For example, the Query goal G2.1 becomes Task AT1 (“Compute Storage Order”) executed at the control or storage system.  
 • The Perform goals become tasks such as AT2 (send resource request), AT3 (battery recharge and reassign), AT4 (deliver resource), AT5 (return resource to checkpoint), AT6 (assign remaining delivery), and AT7 (trigger alert/report).  
 • Each task is annotated with its location (e.g. “Storage”, “Recharging Station”, “Specified Delivery Location”, “Checkpoint”, “Mission Control”, “Sector Manager Office”) and the number of robots required (in most cases “1” because the fallback actions are performed by the original robot or by a reassigning mechanism).

4. Relationships and Runtime Operators  
 • The top-level goal (G1) is an Achieve goal with a target condition that all resource missions must complete (AND relationship). In cases with multiple resources these missions run in parallel (“#”).  
 • Under each resource mission (e.g. G1.1) the Collection and Delivery phases are executed sequentially (using “;”).  
 • Within the Collection phase, the primary plan is sequential (G2.1 then G2.2) but there is a fallback alternative (G2.3) if battery is low.  
 • Within the Delivery phase, the primary plan is G3.1 but if battery is low the fallback branch (G3.2) is executed sequentially and itself uses a fallback if returning to the checkpoint fails.  

